// Up Close spreads for the deadliest foods (level 5) and the everyday foods, keyed by rank: a true story, how the
// poison works, myths, and treatment. Researched 2026-11-14; sources are listed per food.
const UPCLOSE = {
50: {"story": {"title": "Two Mangoes, One Rash (2019)", "text": "In 2019, doctors at an army hospital in San Antonio, Texas, wrote about a 41-year-old man with a mystery rash. Two days after he ate two mangoes and handled the peels, an itchy red rash spread over his body, arms, and legs. His wife ate mangoes the same day and was fine. So why him? Two years earlier, he had a rash from poison ivy. Doctors think that taught his body to attack the very similar chemicals in mango skin. Medicine calmed the itching, and his rash was almost gone within a week."}, "howTitle": "How the rash happens", "how": ["1. Mango skin or sap touches your lips or hands. Its oily chemicals soak into the skin.", "2. They stick to proteins in your skin cells and change their shape.", "3. If poison ivy taught your body to watch for them, <b>immune cells</b> (ih-MYOON) see invaders.", "4. Those cells attack your own skin. A day or two later, it turns red, itchy, and blistery."], "myths": [{"claim": "If mango skin gives you a rash, you can never eat mango.", "truth": false, "explain": "The fruit inside has little or none of the itchy chemicals. Many people are fine if someone else peels it."}, {"claim": "Mango rash can spread from person to person.", "truth": false, "explain": "It isn't caused by germs, so it isn't catching. Only the chemicals from the skin and sap cause it."}, {"claim": "Mango sap is worse than the fruit's skin.", "truth": true, "explain": "The sap that drips from snapped stems has the most itchy chemicals. Mango pickers often get the worst rashes."}], "doctors": "Most mango rashes are mild. Doctors tell people to wash the skin with soap and water and use cool, wet cloths. They may suggest anti-itch creams or medicines called <b>antihistamines</b> (an-tee-HIST-uh-meens). For a bad rash, they give steroid creams or pills to calm the swelling. In the rare case of a big allergic reaction, they give emergency medicine right away.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://westjem.com/case-report/mango-dermatitis-after-urushiol-sensitization.html", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8621529/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urushiol"]},
49: {"story": {"title": "The Butterfish Party (1999)", "text": "In November 1999, about 80 guests went to a party at a restaurant in Victoria, Australia. The main course was crumbed, fried fish called \"butterfish.\" About two and a half hours after the meal, 11 guests had stomach pain and runny, oily diarrhea. Health officers asked the guests what they ate. The butterfish was the link. Tests on fish from the seller showed it wasn't true butterfish at all. It was escolar, or a close cousin. The chefs had no idea. Most of the sick guests felt better within a day."}, "howTitle": "How the oil upsets your gut", "how": ["1. You eat escolar. Its meat is full of <b>wax esters</b>, a waxy kind of oil.", "2. Your gut has fat-cutting <b>enzymes</b> (EN-zymes), but they can't break wax esters apart.", "3. The oil can't soak into your body. It slides through and pools at the end of the gut.", "4. It leaks out as oily orange droplets or diarrhea. Most people are fine in a day or two."], "myths": [{"claim": "Cooking or freezing escolar breaks down the oil.", "truth": false, "explain": "Heat and cold don't change wax esters. Cooked escolar can still cause the same oily trouble."}, {"claim": "Everyone who eats escolar gets sick.", "truth": false, "explain": "Many people feel nothing. At that 1999 party, most of the guests were fine."}, {"claim": "Your body can't digest escolar's oil at all.", "truth": true, "explain": "That's right. Wax esters pass straight through, which is why the oil comes out the other end."}], "doctors": "Most people don't need a doctor. The trouble goes away by itself in a day or two. Doctors tell people to drink lots of water so they don't dry out. If someone has a lot of diarrhea, a doctor may give fluids through an IV. Doctors also ask which fish was eaten, and may tell health officials so the wrong label gets fixed.", "lookalikes": "On a plate, escolar's pale meat looks like white tuna or other mild white fish. Its cousin, the oilfish, looks and acts almost the same.", "sources": ["https://www1.health.gov.au/internet/main/Publishing.nsf/Content/cda-pubs-cdi-2002-cdi2603-htm-cdi2603j.htm", "https://www1.health.gov.au/internet/main/Publishing.nsf/Content/cda-pubs-cdi-2002-cdi2603-htm-cdi2603i.htm", "https://www.cfs.gov.hk/english/multimedia/multimedia_pub/multimedia_pub_fsf_07_01.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escolar"]},
46: {"story": {"title": "Nutmegs Drying in the Sun (2024)", "text": "In Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, a grandmother set some fresh nutmegs outside to dry. They were still wrapped in their red, lacy covering called mace. Her 6-year-old granddaughter peeled off the mace and ate it. That evening, the girl became very sleepy and hard to wake up. Her muscles were stiff and twitchy. Her older brother or sister told the family what she had eaten, and she was rushed to a hospital. Doctors gave her fluids and watched her heart closely. By the next day she was back to normal. Her doctors shared her story in 2024 to warn other families."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Someone eats a lot. <b>Myristicin</b> and <b>elemicin</b> (EL-uh-MISS-in) slowly soak into the blood.", "2. The liver changes some of them into chemicals that act on the brain.", "3. They scramble the brain's messengers. People feel dizzy, confused, or see strange things.", "4. They also make the heart race and the mouth dry. It can last a day or more."], "myths": [{"claim": "Nutmeg is a nut, so people with nut allergies must avoid it.", "truth": false, "explain": "Nutmeg is really a seed. Most people with tree nut allergies can eat it, but ask a doctor first."}, {"claim": "The pinch of nutmeg in a pie or eggnog is dangerous.", "truth": false, "explain": "A small pinch used in cooking is far too little to cause harm."}, {"claim": "Mace, the red covering, has the same chemicals as nutmeg.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. Mace has myristicin too. That's why eating a lot of mace made the girl in Riyadh sick."}], "doctors": "There is no antidote. Doctors keep the person in a calm, quiet room and watch the heart and blood pressure. If the person came in soon after eating, they may give activated charcoal to soak up what's left in the gut. They give fluids, and calming medicines if the person is very upset or shaky. Almost everyone is back to normal within a day or two.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11754422/", "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002899.htm", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4057546/"]},
45: {"story": {"title": "The Thunderclap Headache (2018)", "text": "In 2018, doctors in Cooperstown, New York, wrote about a 34-year-old man who ate one Carolina Reaper at a hot-pepper-eating contest. Right away, he started dry heaving. Over the next few days, his neck hurt badly, and terrible headaches hit him out of nowhere. Each one lasted only a few seconds. Doctors call these <b>thunderclap headaches</b>. A scan showed that some blood vessels in his brain had squeezed narrow. The doctors couldn't be sure, but they thought the pepper was the cause. He got better on his own, and five weeks later his scan was normal."}, "howTitle": "How the heat works", "how": ["1. You bite in. <b>Capsaicin</b> (cap-SAY-ih-sin) touches heat sensors in your mouth called <b>TRPV1</b> (trip-V-one).", "2. It switches them on, so your brain gets a \"fire!\" alarm even though nothing is hot.", "3. The body tries to cool off and clean out. You sweat, cry, drool, and may throw up.", "4. In huge amounts, capsaicin may make blood vessels squeeze tight, which can strain the heart or brain."], "myths": [{"claim": "A big glass of water puts out the burn.", "truth": false, "explain": "Capsaicin is oily, so water just spreads it around. Milk works better because it helps wash it away."}, {"claim": "The seeds are the hottest part of a pepper.", "truth": false, "explain": "Most of the capsaicin is in the white ribs inside, where the seeds attach. The seeds just pick some up."}, {"claim": "Pepper burn on your hands can last for hours.", "truth": true, "explain": "Capsaicin clings to skin and isn't easy to wash off. Touching your eyes later can spread the burn."}], "doctors": "Most people just need time, and the burning fades. For bad stomach pain or throwing up, doctors give fluids and medicines to settle the stomach. If someone has chest pain or a sudden, terrible headache, doctors check the heart with an <b>ECG</b> (a heart tracing) and may scan the brain. Burning skin or eyes are rinsed well. Most people recover fully.", "lookalikes": "It looks a lot like other wrinkly red super-hot peppers, like the ghost pepper and the Trinidad Moruga Scorpion. Without a label, it's hard to tell how hot a pepper is.", "sources": ["https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/carolina-reaper-pepper-causes-thunderclap-headache-doctors-say-n864056", "https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2018/04/180409185313.htm", "https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2021/press-release/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capsaicin"]},
43: {"story": {"title": "Chili at a Military Base (2018)", "text": "On 5 July 2018, lunch at a military dining hall in Brittany, France, included chili con carne. About 1,700 people could have eaten there that day. Within hours, around 200 of them had stomach pain and diarrhea. Some got sick in less than an hour! Health detectives worked for months to solve the case. They found the kidney beans had been added raw, soaked only briefly, and cooked slowly at a low heat. Lab tests found the bean toxin in the chili. Luckily, people got better fast, most within about ten hours."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. You eat undercooked beans. The toxin gets through your stomach acid without breaking.", "2. It's a <b>lectin</b> (LEK-tin), a sticky protein. It grabs sugars on cells lining your gut.", "3. It hurts those lining cells and irritates the gut wall.", "4. Your gut tries to flush it out fast, so throwing up and diarrhea start within hours."], "myths": [{"claim": "Bean poisoning is caused by germs.", "truth": false, "explain": "It's a natural protein in the bean, not bacteria. That's why it can start so fast."}, {"claim": "Soaking beans is enough to make them safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "Soaking softens beans but doesn't destroy the toxin. Only boiling hard does that."}, {"claim": "Fully cooked kidney beans are safe and healthy.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. Hard boiling breaks the toxin apart. Cooked kidney beans are full of protein and fiber."}], "doctors": "There is no antidote, but people usually get better on their own within hours. Doctors make sure the person drinks enough fluids, or give fluids through an IV if they have lost a lot of water. They may give medicine to stop the throwing up. If many people get sick at once, doctors tell health officials, who look for the cause.", "lookalikes": "Dried kidney beans look just like the canned kind. But canned beans are already cooked, while dried ones are raw.", "sources": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10467087/", "https://www.foodsafetynews.com/2023/08/plant-toxin-in-undercooked-kidney-beans-behind-french-outbreak", "https://www.fda.gov/food/foodborne-illness-contaminants/natural-toxins", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phytohaemagglutinin"]},
40: {"story": {"title": "Grapefruit for Breakfast (2004)", "text": "In Berlin, Germany, a 40-year-old woman had taken a cholesterol medicine called <b>simvastatin</b> (sim-vuh-STAT-in) for two years with no trouble. She was healthy and went to the gym. Then she started eating one grapefruit every day. About ten days later, her muscles began to ache and feel weak. Soon her legs were so weak that she went to the hospital. Doctors found her muscles were breaking down. They stopped her medicine and gave her lots of fluids. She got better and went home six days later. Her doctors reported her case in 2004."}, "howTitle": "How grapefruit changes medicines", "how": ["1. You eat grapefruit. Its <b>furanocoumarins</b> (fyur-an-oh-KOO-muh-rins) reach the wall of your gut.", "2. There they break an <b>enzyme</b> (EN-zyme) that normally destroys part of some medicines.", "3. Now much more of the medicine slips into the blood, like taking an extra-big dose.", "4. Too much medicine causes side effects, like muscle damage or an uneven heartbeat."], "myths": [{"claim": "Only grapefruit juice is a problem, not the fruit.", "truth": false, "explain": "Eating the fruit can cause the same effect. The woman in Berlin ate whole grapefruits, not juice."}, {"claim": "Grapefruit makes every medicine stronger.", "truth": false, "explain": "Most medicines are fine. A few become stronger, and a small number actually work less well."}, {"claim": "Older adults are at the most risk.", "truth": true, "explain": "Older people take more medicines and buy the most grapefruit. Their bodies may also handle extra medicine less well."}], "doctors": "Doctors ask about everything the person ate and every medicine they take. They may pause the medicine and tell the person to stop eating grapefruit. Blood tests check for muscle damage and kidney trouble, and IV fluids help protect the kidneys. If a heart medicine is involved, they watch the heartbeat on a monitor. Later, they may switch to a medicine that grapefruit doesn't affect.", "lookalikes": "Pomelos look like extra-big grapefruits and can affect some of the same medicines.", "sources": ["https://i-base.info/htb/9758", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3589309/", "https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/grapefruit-juice-and-some-drugs-dont-mix", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grapefruit%E2%80%93drug_interactions"]},
39: {"story": {"title": "Leaves Cooked Like Cabbage (1917)", "text": "In 1917, during World War I, Britain was short of food. A minister living north of London tried a new vegetable: rhubarb leaves, cooked like cabbage. Soon he became very ill and was taken to a small hospital in Enfield. Sadly, he died five days later. At the inquest, a court hearing about his death, a chemist tested leaves from the same garden. They were full of oxalic acid. The coroner said the case mattered, because many people were trying new foods to save money. Newspapers as far away as New Zealand warned readers."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. You eat the leaves. <b>Oxalic acid</b> (ok-SAL-ik) stings the mouth, throat, and stomach.", "2. In the blood, it grabs calcium and makes tiny crystals of <b>calcium oxalate</b> (OK-suh-late).", "3. With less calcium in the blood, muscles can twitch and the heartbeat can go wrong.", "4. The crystals can clog the kidneys' tiny filters, so the kidneys may stop working well."], "myths": [{"claim": "Cooking rhubarb leaves makes them safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "Cooking doesn't get rid of oxalic acid. The man in 1917 cooked his leaves first."}, {"claim": "Rhubarb stalks are poisonous too.", "truth": false, "explain": "Stalks have only small amounts of oxalic acid. Cooked stalks are safe in normal amounts."}, {"claim": "You can put rhubarb leaves in a compost pile.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. The oxalic acid breaks down quickly as the leaves rot, so the compost is safe for gardens."}], "doctors": "Doctors watch breathing, heartbeat, and blood pressure, and test the blood and urine. They may give activated charcoal and lots of IV fluids to help flush the kidneys. If calcium in the blood drops, they can give more. If the kidneys stop working, a <b>dialysis</b> (dy-AL-ih-sis) machine cleans the blood until they heal. Most people recover in a few days.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://gardenhistoryresearchfoundation.com/tag/rhubarb/", "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002876.htm", "https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/faq/are-rhubarb-leaves-safe-put-compost-pile", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhubarb"]},
38: {"story": {"title": "The Elderberry Wine Play (1941)", "text": "In January 1941, a funny play called <i>Arsenic and Old Lace</i> opened on Broadway in New York. In it, two sweet old aunts named Abby and Martha offer glasses of homemade elderberry wine to lonely old men who come to visit. The joke is that the aunts have secretly added poison to the wine! The play was a huge hit. It ran for 1,444 shows, and in 1944 it became a movie starring Cary Grant. But here is the twist. In the story, the danger wasn't the elderberries at all. It was what the aunts mixed in."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Seeds, leaves, stems, and unripe berries hold <b>cyanogenic glycosides</b>. At first, these chemicals are locked up and harmless.", "2. Crushing and digesting them unlocks the chemicals. They let out a little <b>cyanide</b> (SY-uh-nide).", "3. Cyanide stops cells from using oxygen to make energy. The stomach and gut get upset first.", "4. The body can clear small amounts by itself. That's why most people get sick but recover."], "myths": [{"claim": "Cooking ripe elderberries makes them safe to eat.", "truth": true, "explain": "Heat breaks down the chemicals. That's why syrups, jams, and pies use cooked, ripe berries with the stems removed."}, {"claim": "If birds can eat raw elderberries, people can too.", "truth": false, "explain": "Birds' bodies handle plant chemicals differently. Many berries that birds love can make people sick."}, {"claim": "If a raw elderberry tastes fine, it must be safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "You can't taste the danger. Raw berries can taste okay and still make you throw up."}], "doctors": "Most people only need care for their upset stomach. Doctors give fluids, sometimes through an IV, to replace water lost from throwing up and diarrhea. They may give medicine to settle the stomach, and they check the blood and breathing. For rare, very serious cyanide cases, there are antidotes, such as a form of vitamin B12 called <b>hydroxocobalamin</b> (hy-DROX-oh-koh-BAL-uh-meen). Most people recover quickly.", "lookalikes": "Devil's walking stick has big bunches of dark purple berries that look like elderberries. But its trunk and stems are covered in sharp spines, and elder has none.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenic_and_Old_Lace_(play)", "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/european-elder", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanide_poisoning", "https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/aralia-spinosa/"], "storyLabel": "A famous story"},
37: {"story": {"title": "The Potato's Champion (1772)", "text": "Long ago, many people in France didn't trust potatoes. Some thought they were only good for feeding pigs. Potatoes are cousins of poisonous nightshade plants, and their leaves really are poisonous. A French army chemist named Antoine-Augustin Parmentier helped change minds. As a prisoner of war in Prussia, part of today's Germany, he had eaten potatoes and stayed healthy. Back home, he worked hard to prove they were safe. In 1772, the top medical school in Paris declared potatoes fit to eat. Parmentier held potato dinners for famous guests, like Benjamin Franklin. Today, some French potato dishes are still named after him."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Light and sprouting make potatoes build up <b>solanine</b> (SOH-luh-neen), mostly in and just under the skin.", "2. In the gut, solanine harms the thin outer walls of cells, called <b>membranes</b> (MEM-braynz).", "3. Damaged gut cells cause stomach pain, throwing up, and diarrhea, often hours later.", "4. In big amounts, some reaches the blood and upsets nerve signals. That can cause confusion or a slow heartbeat."], "myths": [{"claim": "Boiling potatoes washes out all the solanine.", "truth": false, "explain": "Some leaks into the cooking water, but much stays in. Experts say not to reuse water that potatoes were boiled in."}, {"claim": "Potato leaves can be cooked and eaten like spinach.", "truth": false, "explain": "The leaves, stems, and little green fruits all have solanine. Only the potato itself is food."}, {"claim": "Little kids should be extra careful with potato skins.", "truth": true, "explain": "Most solanine sits in or near the peel. German food experts say small children should eat peeled potatoes."}], "doctors": "There is no antidote. Doctors give fluids, often through an IV, to replace water lost from throwing up and diarrhea. They may give medicine to calm the stomach. In serious cases, they watch the heartbeat on a monitor and can give medicine if it gets too slow. They also check for confusion. Most people feel better within a few days.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine-Augustin_Parmentier", "https://www.bfr.bund.de/en/faq/solanine__glycoalkaloids__in_potatoes-204310.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solanine"]},
36: {"story": {"title": "The Cookie Dough Recall (2009)", "text": "In the spring of 2009, health detectives in the US noticed something strange. People in 30 states were sick with the same kind of <i>E. coli</i>. Most were under 19, and most were girls and women. When asked what they had eaten, many gave the same answer: raw cookie dough from the store, eaten straight from the package. In June, the company recalled about 3.6 million packages. By June 30, 72 sick people had been counted. Thirty-four went to the hospital, and ten got a serious kidney illness. Thankfully, no one died. Scientists later suspected the flour."}, "howTitle": "How the germs make you sick", "how": ["1. Germs ride along in raw flour or raw eggs. Without baking, nothing kills them.", "2. Swallowed germs get past the stomach and grow in the gut over a few days.", "3. Some <i>E. coli</i> make a poison called <b>Shiga toxin</b> (SHEE-guh). It hurts the gut lining, causing cramps and diarrhea.", "4. Sometimes the toxin travels to the kidneys. That can cause <b>HUS</b>, a serious kidney illness, mostly in young kids."], "myths": [{"claim": "Store-bought dough is safe to eat raw because it comes from a factory.", "truth": false, "explain": "Unless the label says it's made to eat raw, it still needs baking. The 2009 outbreak came from store dough."}, {"claim": "If the dough has no eggs, it's safe to eat raw.", "truth": false, "explain": "Raw flour can carry germs too. Flour is a raw food, just like eggs."}, {"claim": "Young kids are more likely to get the serious kidney illness.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. HUS from <i>E. coli</i> is the top cause of sudden kidney failure in US children."}], "doctors": "Doctors test a stool sample to find the germ. The main treatment is fluids, by drinking or through an IV, to replace lost water. For this kind of <i>E. coli</i>, doctors usually don't give antibiotics, because they may make kidney problems more likely. They check the blood and urine for signs of HUS. If the kidneys stop working, a <b>dialysis</b> (dy-AL-ih-sis) machine cleans the blood until they heal.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/ecoli/2009/cookie-dough-6-30-2009.html", "https://www.livescience.com/36025-cookie-dough-coli-outbreak-mysterious-origins.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escherichia_coli_O157:H7", "https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/handling-flour-safely-what-you-need-know"]},
35: {"story": {"title": "Soup Surprise at School (2009)", "text": "On Friday, 1 May 2009, a class of 30 children at Gorseland Primary School in Suffolk, England, was making soup. They used onions from their very own school garden. But when one child dug up the onions, a daffodil bulb came up too. It went into the soup by mistake. Soon some of the children had stomach cramps and were throwing up. The school quickly called a health helpline and the children's parents. Twelve children were taken to Ipswich Hospital, just to be safe. All of them were allowed to go home later that same afternoon."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Someone eats a bulb, often thinking it's an onion. <b>Lycorine</b> (LY-kor-een) gets to work in the gut.", "2. Lycorine badly upsets the stomach. The body reacts by throwing up, often very fast.", "3. Tiny needle-shaped crystals called <b>oxalates</b> (OX-uh-layts) poke the lips, tongue, and throat, so they burn.", "4. Throwing up gets rid of much of the bulb. That's why most people feel better within hours."], "myths": [{"claim": "Cooking a daffodil bulb makes it safe to eat.", "truth": false, "explain": "Heat doesn't get rid of the poison. Cooked daffodil bulbs still make people sick."}, {"claim": "A daffodil bulb smells just like an onion.", "truth": false, "explain": "A real onion has a strong onion smell and can make your eyes water. A daffodil bulb doesn't."}, {"claim": "Daffodils can make pets sick too.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. Dogs and cats can get sick from eating daffodils, especially the bulbs. Keep them out of reach."}], "doctors": "Doctors first rinse the mouth with water to ease the burning. They give fluids, by mouth or through an IV, to replace water lost from throwing up and diarrhea. They may also give medicine to calm the stomach. If someone ate a lot, doctors check the heartbeat and blood pressure for a while. Most people are well enough to go home the same day.", "lookalikes": "The bulbs look like onions or shallots. The long, flat leaves can be mistaken for leeks or garlic chives.", "sources": ["http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/england/suffolk/8031344.stm", "https://eadt.co.uk/news/21801131.school-children-poison-scare", "https://www.poison.org/articles/daffodils", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7648252/"]},
33: {"story": {"title": "A Stomach Medicine Surprise (1946)", "text": "In the 1940s, a Dutch doctor named F. E. Revers tried a new treatment for stomach ulcers. Those are painful sores inside the stomach. He gave his patients a paste made from licorice root juice. In 1946, he reported that it seemed to help many of them. But he also noticed something odd. About 1 in 5 of his patients got puffy and swollen with extra water, and their blood pressure went up. Other scientists got curious. In 1950, a team in the Netherlands reported that licorice acts like a body hormone that controls salt and water."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Gut germs turn glycyrrhizin into <b>glycyrrhetinic acid</b> (GLISS-er-ruh-TIN-ik), which soaks into the blood.", "2. It blocks a kidney \"guard\" that normally switches off the hormone <b>cortisol</b> (KOR-tih-sol).", "3. Unguarded cortisol tells the kidneys to keep salt and water but get rid of potassium.", "4. Extra water raises blood pressure and causes swelling. Low potassium weakens muscles and upsets the heartbeat."], "myths": [{"claim": "Eating a few pieces of black licorice now and then will hurt your heart.", "truth": false, "explain": "Problems come from eating a lot every day for weeks. A little now and then is fine."}, {"claim": "Anything that tastes like licorice has licorice root in it.", "truth": false, "explain": "Many candies, and plants like anise and fennel, taste similar but have no glycyrrhizin."}, {"claim": "The effects can last after you stop eating licorice.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. It can take days to a few weeks for the body's salt, water, and potassium to get back to normal."}], "doctors": "First, the person stops eating licorice. Doctors run blood tests to check potassium and give extra potassium, by mouth or through an IV. They watch the heartbeat on a monitor, since low potassium can make it uneven. They may give medicine to lower blood pressure and help the body let go of extra water. Most people recover fully.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJM195103292441303", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/endocrinology/articles/10.3389/fendo.2019.00484/full", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glycyrrhizin", "https://read.qxmd.com/read/12640291/an-unusual-cause-of-hypokalemic-paralysis-chronic-licorice-ingestion"]},
32: {"story": {"title": "California's Raw Milk Outbreak (2023)", "text": "In October 2023, health workers in San Diego, California, noticed eight people sick with <i>Salmonella</i> germs. All of them had drunk raw milk from the same dairy farm. The farm stopped making milk for a week and recalled it from stores. Health detectives kept searching and found 171 sick people, almost all in California. Most were children and teens, and half were 7 years old or younger. The youngest was just 9 months old, and the oldest was 87. Twenty-two people needed a hospital. Happily, no one died. The US CDC reported on the outbreak in 2025."}, "howTitle": "How the germs make you sick", "how": ["1. Germs get into milk on the farm, from manure, dirty equipment, or a sick animal's udder.", "2. Cold slows most germs but doesn't kill them. <i>Listeria</i> (lis-TEER-ee-uh) can even grow in the fridge.", "3. In the gut, germs multiply and harm its lining. That causes cramps, diarrhea, and throwing up.", "4. Some germs spread further. They can reach the blood, the kidneys, or an unborn baby."], "myths": [{"claim": "Raw milk helps people who can't digest milk sugar.", "truth": false, "explain": "Raw and pasteurized milk have the same <b>lactose</b> (LAK-tohs), the milk sugar that causes the trouble."}, {"claim": "Pasteurizing milk takes away most of its goodness.", "truth": false, "explain": "Heating barely changes milk's protein, fat, and minerals, and changes most vitamins very little."}, {"claim": "Cheese made from raw milk can carry germs too.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. In the 2023 California outbreak, raw milk cheese aged 60 days still had the outbreak germ."}], "doctors": "Doctors ask what the person ate and drank, and test a stool sample to find the germ. Most people need rest and plenty of fluids, sometimes through an IV. For some germs, like <i>Listeria</i>, doctors give antibiotics quickly, especially for pregnant women and babies. For <i>E. coli</i>, they usually don't. They watch the kidneys of young children closely.", "lookalikes": "Raw milk looks just like ordinary milk. Only the label, which should say \"pasteurized,\" tells you which kind it is.", "sources": ["https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/74/wr/mm7427a1.htm", "https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/raw-milk-misconceptions-and-danger-raw-milk-consumption", "https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/food-safety-and-raw-milk", "https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/raw-milk.html"]},
31: {"story": {"title": "The 17-Month Chicken Outbreak (2013)", "text": "In March 2013, people in the US began getting sick with a kind of <i>Salmonella</i> called Heidelberg. Health detectives traced it to raw chicken from one big company, Foster Farms. The outbreak kept going for about 17 months, until July 2014. In all, 634 people in 29 states and Puerto Rico got sick, mostly in California. Nearly 4 in 10 of them went to the hospital, which is a lot for <i>Salmonella</i>. Many of the germs could resist some antibiotics. Thankfully, no one died. It was a big reminder to always cook chicken all the way through."}, "howTitle": "How the germs make you sick", "how": ["1. <i>Salmonella</i> and <i>Campylobacter</i> live in chickens' guts. They can get onto the meat on farms or in factories.", "2. Undercooked chicken, or raw juice dripping onto other food, carries live germs into your mouth.", "3. In your gut, the germs attack the lining. It swells, causing cramps, diarrhea, and fever.", "4. Rarely, germs get into the blood. Very rarely, <i>Campylobacter</i> triggers <b>Guillain-Barré syndrome</b> (GHEE-yan bah-RAY), a nerve illness."], "myths": [{"claim": "Freezing chicken kills the germs.", "truth": false, "explain": "Freezing only stops germs from growing. They become active again when the chicken thaws. Only cooking kills them."}, {"claim": "Organic or free-range chicken doesn't carry germs.", "truth": false, "explain": "Any raw chicken can carry <i>Salmonella</i> or <i>Campylobacter</i>, however it was raised."}, {"claim": "Germs from raw chicken can spread to a salad on the same cutting board.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yes. That's why cooks use a separate board for raw meat, or wash boards in hot, soapy water."}], "doctors": "Most people get better on their own in about a week. Doctors make sure they drink plenty of fluids, or give fluids through an IV if they are losing too much water. A stool test shows which germ it is. Antibiotics are mostly for very sick people, babies, and older adults. Labs check which antibiotics still work, since some germs resist them.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/salmonella/heidelberg-10-13/index.html", "https://www.foodsafetynews.com/after-17-months-foster-farms-salmonella-outbreak-declared-over/", "https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/chicken.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campylobacteriosis"]},
24: {"story": {"title": "Salad Day at Summer Camp (1980)", "text": "On July 11, 1980, a big day camp in Passaic County, New Jersey, had a \"nature group\" that tried a wild salad. A counselor had made it with young pokeweed leaves, picked that morning and boiled twice. The counselor had made this salad for years without trouble. This time, about 3 hours after eating, campers began to feel sick. Twenty-one people got sick, with stomach cramps, dizziness, and throwing up. Eighteen were checked by doctors, and four stayed in the hospital for a day or two. Health officials wrote up the case so others would learn from it."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. You eat it. Its <b>saponins</b> (SAP-oh-nins) work like a harsh soap on the gut.", "2. They irritate the lining of the stomach and gut, which burns and cramps.", "3. The body tries to get rid of it fast, by throwing up and diarrhea.", "4. Losing lots of water can drop blood pressure. In rare cases, seizures can happen."], "myths": [{"claim": "If birds can eat the berries, people can too.", "truth": false, "explain": "Birds handle the poison differently. The same berries can make children sick."}, {"claim": "Boiling the leaves always makes them safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "Even leaves boiled twice made campers sick in 1980. Experts say no cooking method is a guarantee."}, {"claim": "Pokeweed juice can get into your body through cuts.", "truth": true, "explain": "Sap getting into broken skin can affect the blood. That's why people wear gloves to pull it up."}], "doctors": "There is no antidote, but most people get better within a day or two. Doctors give fluids through an IV to replace water lost from throwing up and diarrhea. They may give medicine to calm the stomach. They check blood pressure and the heartbeat. In rare serious cases, they treat seizures and watch the patient closely in the hospital.", "lookalikes": "The dark purple berry clusters can look like small grapes or blueberries to young children.", "sources": ["https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/1236", "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002874.htm", "https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/phytolacca-americana/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phytolacca_americana"]},
22: {"story": {"title": "A Warning from Tokyo (2017)", "text": "In early 2017, a family in Tokyo, Japan, gave their 6-month-old baby boy honey mixed into juice, twice a day. They didn't know honey isn't safe for babies. After about a month, he started coughing. In February, he had seizures and trouble breathing, and an ambulance took him to the hospital. Doctors found infant botulism. Sadly, he died at the end of March. Tests found the germs in the family's open honey jar. Officials said it was Japan's first known death from infant botulism since 1986, and they reminded families: no honey before age 1."}, "howTitle": "How the germs make you sick", "how": ["1. A baby swallows honey with tiny, sleeping germ spores in it.", "2. A baby's gut doesn't have grown-up gut germs yet, so the spores wake up and grow.", "3. The new germs make <b>botulinum toxin</b> (BOT-yoo-LY-num), which soaks into the blood.", "4. The toxin blocks signals from nerves to muscles. Muscles go floppy, even breathing muscles."], "myths": [{"claim": "Baking honey into food kills the spores.", "truth": false, "explain": "The spores are very tough. Normal baking doesn't kill them, so baked foods with honey aren't safe for babies."}, {"claim": "Raw or organic honey is safe for babies.", "truth": false, "explain": "Any kind of honey can carry spores. No honey of any kind before a baby's first birthday."}, {"claim": "Honey is safe for kids over 1 year old.", "truth": true, "explain": "By then, gut germs stop the spores from growing. Older kids and grown-ups can enjoy honey."}], "doctors": "Doctors give a special medicine made of antibodies, which grab the toxin and stop it from doing more harm. The baby may need a feeding tube, and some need a breathing machine until their muscles get strong again. This can take weeks. Doctors usually avoid antibiotics, which can make things worse. With good care, most babies recover fully.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2017/04/10/national/infant-botulism-claims-6-month-old-adachi-ward-boy-fed-honey/", "https://www.cdc.gov/botulism/hcp/clinical-overview/infant-botulism.html", "https://www.cdc.gov/botulism/prevention/index.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botulism"]},
20: {"story": {"title": "The Inventor to the Rescue (2016)", "text": "In May 2016, Dr. Henry Heimlich was 96 years old. He lived in a senior home in Cincinnati, Ohio. One evening at dinner, the woman sitting next to him, Patty Ris, who was 87, began to choke on a piece of meat. Dr. Heimlich stood up, got behind her, and pushed in and up on her belly, just as he had taught the world. After a few pushes, the food popped out, and she could breathe again. He said it was the first time he had used his own move to save someone. She later wrote him a thank-you note."}, "howTitle": "How choking happens", "how": ["1. A small child bites a whole grape but can't chew it well yet.", "2. The smooth, round grape slips to the back of the throat.", "3. It fits the <b>windpipe</b> (also called the <b>trachea</b>, TRAY-kee-uh) like a cork in a bottle.", "4. The squishy grape seals the tube tight, so no air gets past to cough it out."], "myths": [{"claim": "Cutting a grape in half across the middle makes it safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "The halves can still be round plugs. For kids 5 and under, cut grapes lengthwise into quarters."}, {"claim": "A choking person always coughs loudly.", "truth": false, "explain": "Bad choking is often silent. A person who can't cough, talk, or breathe needs help right away."}, {"claim": "If a child is coughing hard, let them keep coughing.", "truth": true, "explain": "A strong cough is the best way to clear the throat. A grown-up should stay close and watch."}], "doctors": "If food is still stuck when a child reaches the hospital, doctors may use a thin tube with a tiny camera, called a <b>bronchoscope</b> (BRON-koh-skope), to see the food and pull it out. They give oxygen and check the lungs. They watch for infection or swelling afterward. Children who get help quickly usually recover completely.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://www.statnews.com/2016/05/27/heimlich-maneuver/", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2016/5/28/dr-heimlich-does-the-heimlich-after-hamburger-choking", "https://www.nhs.uk/baby/first-aid-and-safety/first-aid/how-to-stop-a-child-from-choking/", "https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/712637"]},
19: {"story": {"title": "Oysters in Sarasota (2018)", "text": "In July 2018, a 71-year-old man ate raw oysters at a restaurant in Sarasota, Florida. He already had some health problems. Soon after, he became very sick. Two days later, he died. The Florida Department of Health said the cause was <b>Vibrio vulnificus</b>. A local health official said the man had not been swimming in the sea, so the germs must have come from the oysters. Health officials reminded people that these infections are rare, but very serious for people with weak immune systems. They said the safest choice is to eat oysters cooked."}, "howTitle": "How the germs make you sick", "how": ["1. You eat a raw oyster with Vibrio germs. Some germs survive your stomach acid.", "2. They slip through the gut wall into the blood, and multiply fast.", "3. They make toxins that harm cells. Blood vessels leak and blood pressure drops.", "4. This is called <b>septic shock</b> (SEP-tik). Skin blisters, and organs can stop working."], "myths": [{"claim": "You can tell a germy oyster by its look or smell.", "truth": false, "explain": "Oysters with Vibrio look, smell, and taste just like safe ones. There is no way to tell."}, {"claim": "Oysters from clean water don't have Vibrio.", "truth": false, "explain": "Vibrio isn't pollution. It lives naturally in warm, salty coastal water, even very clean water."}, {"claim": "Most people who eat raw oysters never get sick from Vibrio.", "truth": true, "explain": "That's true. But for people with liver disease or weak immune systems, one infection can be deadly."}], "doctors": "Speed is everything. Doctors start strong antibiotics right away, often before tests come back. Patients usually go to the intensive care unit, where they get fluids through an IV and medicines to raise blood pressure. If skin and muscle are badly harmed, surgeons may need to clean out the damaged areas. Fast care saves many lives.", "lookalikes": "", "sources": ["https://www.cnbc.com/2018/07/19/man-dies-bacterial-infection-ate-tainted-oyster-at-florida-restaurant.html", "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/florida-man-dies-vibrio-bacterial-infection-after-eating-raw-oysters/", "https://www.cdc.gov/vibrio/about/index.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vibrio_vulnificus"]},
13: {"story": {"title": "A Flower Becomes a Medicine (1880s)", "text": "For a long time, ordinary people in Russia used lily of the valley as a home remedy for swelling. Around 1880, Russian doctors began to study it. They found it acted on the heart, a lot like foxglove. By 1883, doctors in other countries were trying it too. A doctor in Rome wrote about it in the <i>British Medical Journal</i>. In 1900, it was added to the official list of American medicines. But its strength was hard to control. Doctors stopped using it, and today there's no proof it helps treat any illness."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. You eat it. Heart poisons like <b>convallatoxin</b> soak into the blood, though usually not much.", "2. They jam a tiny pump in heart cells, the <b>sodium-potassium pump</b> (SOH-dee-um poh-TASS-ee-um).", "3. Heart cells fill with too much salt and calcium, so their electric signals go wrong.", "4. The heartbeat can get very slow or uneven. In bad cases, the heart can stop."], "myths": [{"claim": "Smelling the flowers can poison you.", "truth": false, "explain": "The sweet smell is harmless. That's why it's popular in perfume. The danger is eating the plant."}, {"claim": "Every nibble causes serious heart trouble.", "truth": false, "explain": "The body soaks up only a little of the poison, so most accidental nibbles cause mild or no sickness."}, {"claim": "Dogs and cats can be poisoned by it too.", "truth": true, "explain": "Pets can get heart trouble from it, just like people. Keep it out of their reach."}], "doctors": "Doctors put the patient on a heart monitor and do blood tests, including checks of potassium. If the plant was eaten very recently, they may give activated charcoal. They give atropine for a slow heartbeat. In serious cases, they may give an antidote made for foxglove-type poisons. Most people feel better within about a day.", "lookalikes": "In Europe, its spring leaves are mistaken for wild garlic. In North America, they can be confused with ramps, also called wild leeks.", "sources": ["https://henriettes-herb.com/eclectic/lloyd-hist/convallaria.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lily_of_the_valley", "https://www3.ha.org.hk/toxicplant/en/convallaria_majalis.html", "https://www.aliem.com/acmt-toxicology-visual-pearl-heart-wont-go-on/"]},
11: {"story": {"title": "The Wrong Tea for Sleep (2016)", "text": "In 2016, a 63-year-old woman in England had trouble sleeping. A friend told her a tea made from comfrey leaves might help. She bought leaves at a local market and made tea at home. A few hours later, she was throwing up, her heart was pounding, and she nearly fainted. At the hospital, her heartbeat was slow and uneven. Blood tests showed heart poison from foxglove. The leaves she bought were not comfrey at all. Doctors gave her an antidote. After five days, her heart was back to normal, and she went home."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. You eat it. Poisons like <b>digitoxin</b> (DIJ-ih-TOK-sin) move from the gut into the blood.", "2. They block a tiny pump in heart cells that controls salt and calcium.", "3. The heart squeezes harder, but the signals that set its rhythm slow down and misfire.", "4. The heartbeat gets very slow or uneven. Without help, it can stop."], "myths": [{"claim": "Drying foxglove leaves makes them safe.", "truth": false, "explain": "Dried leaves still have the poison. That's why tea made from dried leaves can be so dangerous."}, {"claim": "Just touching foxglove can stop your heart.", "truth": false, "explain": "Touching it is very unlikely to harm you, but wear gloves and wash your hands after gardening."}, {"claim": "Foxglove leaves taste very bitter.", "truth": true, "explain": "They do. The bitter taste often stops people from eating much, which is one reason deaths are rare."}], "doctors": "Doctors check the heart with a monitor and test the blood for the poison and for potassium. If the plant was eaten recently, they may give activated charcoal. The main antidote is made of tiny antibody pieces that grab the poison and carry it out of the body. Most people recover.", "lookalikes": "Before it flowers, foxglove's leaves get mixed up with comfrey, which some people use for tea. Leaves sold as comfrey have even turned out to be foxglove.", "sources": ["https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/729182", "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2016-12-mix-up-homemade-herbal-tea-woman.html", "https://www.poison.org/articles/foxglove", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digitalis_purpurea"]},
10: {"story": {"title": "From Poison to Medicine (1962)", "text": "In August 1962, a plant scientist named Arthur Barclay was collecting samples in a forest in Washington state, USA. His job was to send bits of plants to the US National Cancer Institute, where scientists tested them on cancer cells. One sample was bark from a Pacific yew. Two chemists, Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani, found it held a strong new chemical. In 1971 they named it <b>taxol</b>. Years later, in 1992, it was approved as a cancer medicine. Today it is made without cutting down wild yews, and it has helped a great many patients."}, "howTitle": "How the poison works", "how": ["1. Chewed seeds or needles let out <b>taxine</b> (TAK-seen), which soaks into the blood fast.", "2. Taxine blocks tiny gates on heart cells that let salt and calcium in and out.", "3. The heart's electric signals slow down, so the beat gets slow and weak.", "4. Blood pressure falls. The heartbeat can go wild or stop, sometimes within hours."], "myths": [{"claim": "The red flesh is the most poisonous part.", "truth": false, "explain": "The soft red flesh has very little poison. The danger is the hard seed inside, and the needles."}, {"claim": "Dried yew clippings are safe for horses and cows.", "truth": false, "explain": "Dried clippings stay poisonous. Farm animals have died after eating trimmings tossed over a fence."}, {"claim": "Yew needles are poisonous all year round.", "truth": true, "explain": "Yews are evergreens, and their needles keep their poison in every season."}], "doctors": "There is no proven antidote. Doctors watch the heart on a monitor and may give activated charcoal if the plant was eaten recently. They use medicines, or a small wire called a pacemaker, to keep a slow heart beating. Electric shocks can reset a wild rhythm. In the worst cases, a heart-lung machine can take over until the poison wears off.", "lookalikes": "Its flat needles look like those of fir and hemlock trees. The bright red fruit can look like a sweet berry to a child.", "sources": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9656977/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxus_baccata", "https://www.acs.org/education/whatischemistry/landmarks/camptothecintaxol.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paclitaxel"]},
7: {"story":{"title":"The Umbrella Mystery (1978)","text":"In September 1978, a writer named Georgi Markov was waiting for a bus near Waterloo Bridge in London. He felt a sharp sting in his thigh, like a bug bite. Many reports say a stranger jabbed him with a trick umbrella. That night he got a high fever, and four days later he died. Scientists found a tiny metal ball, smaller than a pinhead, in his leg. A court inquiry decided he was killed with <b>ricin</b>, the poison from castor beans. The attacker was never caught."},"how":["1. A chewed seed lets ricin out. It gets into the body from the gut.","2. Ricin sticks to the outside of cells and sneaks inside.","3. It breaks the cell's <b>ribosomes</b> (RY-buh-sohmz), the tiny machines that build proteins.","4. Cells die, first in the gut. Then water loss and organ damage can follow."],"myths":[{"claim":"Castor oil has ricin in it.","truth":false,"explain":"Ricin doesn't mix with oil, and heat during making destroys it. It stays in the leftover seed pulp."},{"claim":"Swallowing a seed whole is as bad as chewing it.","truth":false,"explain":"A hard shell may let a whole seed pass through. Chewed seeds are the big danger. Still, always get help."},{"claim":"Just touching a castor plant can poison you.","truth":false,"explain":"Ricin can't get through healthy skin. But the sap can cause an itchy rash in some people."}],"doctors":"There is no antidote. If the seeds were eaten very recently, doctors may give activated charcoal to soak up poison. They give lots of fluids through an IV to replace lost water, and medicines for low blood pressure or seizures. They watch the liver and kidneys closely. Most people who get good care get better.","lookalikes":"The shiny, speckled seeds look like fancy beans. They also look like ticks! Its name, Ricinus, means \"tick\" in Latin. People sometimes make them into beads.","sources":["https://www.cdc.gov/chemical-emergencies/chemical-fact-sheets/ricin.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricinus","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricin","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgi_Markov"]},
6: {"story":{"title":"A Doctor Tests Hemlock (1860s)","text":"In the 1860s, hemlock was still used as a medicine. A London doctor named John Harley wanted to know exactly what it did. So he took a small, measured dose himself. Within an hour, his heels felt heavy and his legs went weak and shaky. At one point he could barely lift his eyelids. But he wrote that his mind stayed \"perfectly clear and calm.\" A few hours later, he was back to normal. He shared his notes in a book in 1869. Doctors stopped using hemlock long ago, and no one should ever try it."},"how":["1. You eat it. Coniine gets into your blood from your gut, often within 30 minutes.","2. It grabs nerve <b>receptors</b> (rih-SEP-turz) like a huge dose of nicotine, causing shaking.","3. Then it blocks nerve signals to muscles. Weakness starts in the legs and moves up.","4. When the breathing muscles stop working, the person can't breathe without a machine."],"myths":[{"claim":"Hemlock trees are the same poison that killed Socrates.","truth":false,"explain":"Hemlock trees are evergreens, not related at all. They got the name because their crushed leaves smell similar."},{"claim":"Only the seeds are poisonous.","truth":false,"explain":"The leaves, stems, flowers, and seeds are all poisonous. The seeds get stronger late in the summer."},{"claim":"Dead, dried-up hemlock is safe.","truth":false,"explain":"Drying weakens it, but dead hollow stems can stay deadly for up to three years."}],"doctors":"There is no antidote. Doctors may give activated charcoal to soak up poison in the gut, and medicine for drooling. If breathing gets weak, a breathing machine takes over, often for a few days, until the poison wears off. They also check the muscles and kidneys, which can be hurt. With fast care, most people recover.","lookalikes":"Wild carrot (Queen Anne's lace), parsley, parsnip, fennel, and celery leaves. It also looks like water hemlock, an even deadlier plant.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conium_maculatum","https://archive.org/details/oldvegetableneur00harl","https://europepmc.org/article/MED/30594904","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuga"]},
5: {"story":{"title":"The Wrong Root at Dinner (1856)","text":"In January 1856, the Provost (like a mayor) of Dingwall, a town in Scotland, held a dinner party. The cook sent a servant to the garden to dig up horseradish for a sauce to go with the roast beef. By mistake, the servant dug up monkshood roots, which look a lot like horseradish. Three guests, including two priests, got sick during the meal and died soon after. Some others who were affected recovered. The local newspaper reported the sad mix-up, and people still tell it as a warning."},"how":["1. You eat it, or it gets in through a cut. <b>Aconitine</b> soaks into the blood fast.","2. It jams open tiny gates on nerve and muscle cells called <b>sodium channels</b> (SOH-dee-um).","3. Nerves misfire, so the mouth and face tingle and go numb. Muscles get weak.","4. Heart cells misfire too. The heartbeat goes wild and uneven, and the heart can stop."],"myths":[{"claim":"Wolfsbane keeps werewolves away.","truth":false,"explain":"Werewolves are only in stories. The name \"wolfsbane\" comes from people using it to poison real wolves."},{"claim":"Boiling the roots makes monkshood safe.","truth":false,"explain":"Long boiling weakens the poison, but people still get poisoned by herbal remedies that weren't made right."},{"claim":"It can harm you through your skin.","truth":true,"explain":"The poison can get in through skin, especially cuts. That's why gardeners wear gloves."}],"doctors":"There is no antidote. Doctors watch the heartbeat on a monitor all the time. They give atropine for a slow heartbeat and drugs like amiodarone to calm an uneven one. They support blood pressure. In the worst cases, a heart-lung machine can pump blood for the body until the poison wears off.","lookalikes":"Its roots have been mistaken for horseradish. Its leaves have been mixed into salads by people who thought they were picking wild greens.","sources":["https://www.poison.org/articles/why-is-monkshood-considered-a-poison--174","https://europepmc.org/article/MED/19514874","https://electricscotland.com/History/highlands/3no15.htm"]},
4: {"story":{"title":"A Mix-Up in Ithaca (2006)","text":"In July 2006, a man near Ithaca, New York, picked some young white mushrooms in a state park. He thought they were inky caps, a kind people eat. He fried them and ate them. They were really destroying angels. He got very sick and ended up in a big hospital in Rochester. Doctors gave him charcoal, fluids, and medicine, and got ready in case he needed a new liver. Luckily, his liver slowly healed. Later, he wrote his story for Cornell University's mushroom blog to warn others."},"how":["1. You eat it. <b>Amatoxins</b> pass from your gut into your blood. Cooking doesn't stop them.","2. Liver cells soak them up. Inside, the poison jams the cell's gene copy machine.","3. Cells can't make new proteins, so they slowly die. That's why sickness is delayed.","4. The poison keeps cycling back to the liver. The liver and kidneys can fail."],"myths":[{"claim":"A poison mushroom turns a silver spoon black.","truth":false,"explain":"Poison mushrooms have nothing that reacts with silver. This test doesn't work at all."},{"claim":"If animals eat a mushroom, it's safe for people.","truth":false,"explain":"Animals handle poisons differently. Squirrels, rabbits, and slugs can eat deadly Amanitas."},{"claim":"A deadly mushroom will taste bad.","truth":false,"explain":"Our tongues can't sense poison. People who ate deadly mushrooms often said they tasted fine."}],"doctors":"There is no proven antidote. Doctors give lots of IV fluids and may give activated charcoal. Some hospitals use medicines like silibinin (from milk thistle), high-dose penicillin, or NAC to try to protect the liver. They test the liver often. If it fails, a liver transplant can save the person's life.","lookalikes":"Button, meadow, and horse mushrooms. Young ones still wrapped in their \"egg\" look like puffballs. Cutting them in half shows the hidden mushroom shape.","sources":["https://blog.mycology.cornell.edu/?p=68","https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/66/wr/mm6621a1.htm","https://www.toxinfo.ch/toxic_tales_mushroom","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Destroying_angel"]},
3: {"story":{"title":"Captain Cook's Fishy Supper, 1774","text":"On 7 September 1774, explorer Captain James Cook's ship was at New Caledonia, an island in the Pacific. Local people sold the crew a new kind of fish, now thought to be a pufferfish. Cook and two scientists, Johann and Georg Forster, only tasted its liver and eggs. At about 3 a.m. they woke up weak and numb all over. Cook wrote that a feather and a full pot of water felt the same weight in his hand! The three men got better. But a pig that ate the fish's guts was found dead in the morning."},"how":["1. You eat it. The poison moves from your gut into your blood within minutes to hours.","2. It plugs tiny gates on nerves called <b>sodium channels</b> (SOH-dee-um CHAN-uls).","3. Nerve signals stop. Lips go numb, then muscles go floppy, but the mind often stays awake.","4. The breathing muscles stop. Without a breathing machine, the body runs out of oxygen."],"myths":[{"claim":"Cooking fugu makes the poison safe.","truth":false,"explain":"Normal cooking heat does not destroy tetrodotoxin. Poisonous parts stay poisonous, cooked or raw."},{"claim":"Pufferfish make the poison themselves.","truth":false,"explain":"Scientists think it mostly comes from tiny bacteria in their food. Farm fish fed clean food aren't poisonous."},{"claim":"A poisoned person can be awake but unable to move.","truth":true,"explain":"Yes. The mind often stays clear even when the body can't move a finger."}],"doctors":"There is no antidote. If the person comes in early, doctors may give activated charcoal to soak up poison still in the gut. If breathing gets weak, a breathing machine breathes for them. The body slowly clears the poison by itself. People who survive the first day usually recover fully in a few days.","lookalikes":"There are many kinds of pufferfish, and they look alike. Some have poison in their meat and skin too, so even experts find them hard to tell apart.","sources":["https://www.hokeniryo1.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/shokuhin/eng/hugu/index.html","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2525488/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrodotoxin","https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15869/pg15869.html"]},
2: {"story":{"title":"The Wrong Root, Maine 1992","text":"In October 1992, two brothers were hunting for wild ginseng in the woods of Maine, USA. Near a swampy spot they dug up a root they thought was ginseng, and both took bites. It was water hemlock. Within 30 minutes, the younger brother, who ate three bites, was throwing up and having seizures. Rescuers rushed him to a hospital, but he died about 3 hours after eating. His older brother took just one bite. He had seizures too, but doctors treated him and he recovered."},"how":["1. You eat the root. The poison soaks into your blood fast, often in 15 to 60 minutes.","2. It blocks the brain's \"brakes,\" a calming signal system called <b>GABA</b> (GAB-uh).","3. With no brakes, nerves fire nonstop. That causes drooling, vomiting, and violent seizures.","4. Long seizures can stop normal breathing and upset the heartbeat, which can kill."],"myths":[{"claim":"Socrates was killed with water hemlock.","truth":false,"explain":"He drank poison hemlock, a different plant. It's a cousin with spotted stems and a mousy smell."},{"claim":"Drying the root makes it safe.","truth":false,"explain":"Dried water hemlock roots are still poisonous. There's no way to make the plant safe."},{"claim":"One small bite of root can kill an adult.","truth":true,"explain":"Yes. A piece of root only 2 to 3 centimeters long can be deadly for an adult."}],"doctors":"There is no antidote. Doctors may give activated charcoal if the person comes in soon after eating. They keep the airway open, give fluids, and use strong seizure-stopping medicines called benzodiazepines (ben-zoh-dye-AZ-uh-peens). Fast hospital care saves many lives.","lookalikes":"Wild parsnip, wild carrot (Queen Anne's lace), wild ginseng, celery, and parsley. It's also mixed up with its cousin, poison hemlock.","sources":["https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/00026056.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicutoxin","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conium_maculatum"]},
1: {"story":{"title":"California's Death Cap Winter, 2016","text":"In late 2016, a huge crop of death caps popped up in Northern California. Over about two weeks in December, 14 people got sick from eating wild-picked mushrooms. Five of them shared one meal at home. Their stomach trouble started hours later, and some didn't reach a hospital until two days after eating. Doctors gave them fluids and liver medicines. Eleven people recovered. Three needed new livers, including a toddler who had eaten only about half a mushroom cap."},"how":["1. You eat it. The poison passes from your gut to your liver, the body's cleaner.","2. In liver cells it jams <b>RNA polymerase</b> (puh-LIM-er-ays), a machine that reads genes.","3. The cells can't make the proteins they need, so they slowly die. The kidneys get hurt too.","4. After a few days the liver fails. Without fast care or a new liver, this can kill."],"myths":[{"claim":"Poisonous mushrooms taste bad.","truth":false,"explain":"Many deadly mushrooms taste mild or nutty. Death caps have been reported to taste pleasant."},{"claim":"If animals eat a mushroom, it's safe for people.","truth":false,"explain":"Slugs can munch on death caps with little harm. The same poison destroys a human liver."},{"claim":"A silver spoon turns black near a poisonous mushroom.","truth":false,"explain":"Experts call this wishful thinking. No simple home test can tell safe mushrooms from deadly ones."}],"doctors":"There is no proven antidote. Doctors give lots of fluids and may give activated charcoal. They often add medicines to protect the liver, such as silibinin (from milk thistle), N-acetylcysteine, or penicillin, though none is proven to work for sure. If the liver fails, a liver transplant can save a life.","lookalikes":"Edible paddy straw mushrooms (popular in Asia), Caesar's mushrooms, young puffballs, and green-capped russulas. Young death caps look like little white eggs.","sources":["https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/66/wr/mm6621a1.htm","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amanita_phalloides","https://www.toxinfo.ch/toxic_tales_mushroom","https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID:36129244&resultType=core&format=json"]},
};
